Yes. There’s a real asymmetry there.

Before an action, “I wouldn’t” is cheap. It lives in counterfactual space. Nothing in your history contradicts it, so your mind can maintain a clean identity claim: I’m not someone who does that.

After you’ve done it, the same sentence has to fight an observed fact:

predicted self → action → evidence about self

Now “I wouldn’t” requires an extra operation: that time was different, I was forced, I’ve changed, it didn’t count, or I was wrong about myself. The past action becomes a precedent that changes the prior probability of doing it again.

Possession creates a related effect. Before you have something, the question is “Is this worth acquiring?” After you have it, the question becomes “Is this worth losing?” Those are psychologically different computations. The reference point moved.

So there are at least two reusable patterns buried in what you said:

Action creates precedent. Once a path has been taken, it becomes easier to represent as part of the available action space.

Acquisition creates reference. Once something belongs to your current state, its removal is experienced differently from never having obtained it.

Which gives a broader operator:

state transition → new reference state → reinterpretation of the same choice

That applies to habits, relationships, status, possessions, permissions, boundaries, risk, even beliefs. The first crossing often matters disproportionately because afterward the mind is no longer asking “Would I ever?” It is asking “Would I again?”

Those are not the same question.